I've read of an actual test of the hypothesis - not a set-up experiment, bu=
t=20
an observation of two existing groups of people. There is a noticeable=20
difference in the kinds of industrial accidents that Swedish-speaking Finns=
 and=20
Finnish-speaking Finns get into; it was traced to the different ways the tw=
o=20
languages encode spatiotemporal ideas. Finns who learned one language at ho=
me=20
are required to learn the other in school (except in =C5land), so it's not =
just=20
knowing the language that makes the difference, but learning it first.

Lojban has at least ten ways of saying "and", several ways of saying "if", =
a=20
large set of specific prepositions, both spatial and temporal tenses, and f=
ull=20
clusivity. On the other hand, it lacks grammatical number and gender. What=
